Title:
Born to Defend, Weaponized to Kill: Weaponizing Microsoft’s Own Driver to Kill EDRs

Type:
Blog Post

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
- The post reverse engineers a Microsoft Defender-signed kernel driver (KSLDriver.sys) that exposes a process-termination primitive usable from user mode via a single IOCTL.  
- It shows how the driver’s only “authentication” is a registry-configured `AllowedProcessName` string comparison, which an admin can modify before starting the driver service, enabling arbitrary callers.  
- The kill workflow uses IOCTL `0x222044` (sub-command 8) to store a target `EPROCESS`, then triggers termination on `IRP_MJ_CLEANUP` when the device handle is closed, calling `ZwTerminateProcess` from ring 0.  
- The author highlights that Microsoft-signed drivers are excluded from the vulnerable driver blocklist, making this a BYOVD-style technique without third-party drivers.  
- A proof-of-concept tool (“DefenderKiller”) is provided to load the driver, set registry values, open the device, send the kill command, and close the handle to terminate protected/security processes.  
- Useful primarily for red teams and offensive security researchers studying EDR tampering and kernel-assisted process killing; also relevant to defenders for understanding a trust boundary failure around signed drivers and device ACLs.

Technical Focus:
- BYOVD using Microsoft-signed Defender kernel driver (KSLDriver.sys)
- IOCTL interface abuse (`0x222044`, sub-command dispatch)
- Registry-controlled access control (`AllowedProcessName`, `DeviceName`, `ImagePath`)
- IRP handlers and kill-on-close behavior (`IRP_MJ_CREATE`, `IRP_MJ_DEVICE_CONTROL`, `IRP_MJ_CLEANUP`)
- Kernel process termination path (`ZwOpenProcess`, `ObReferenceObjectByHandle`, `ZwTerminateProcess`)
- Device creation security (`IoCreateDeviceSecure`, SDDL)

Use Cases:
- Terminate EDR/AV user-mode processes (including PPL) during authorized engagements
- Study/replicate kernel-assisted process kill primitives for research and detection engineering
- Validate defensive controls around driver loading, service registry hardening, and device access monitoring
- Build detections for suspicious IOCTL usage and registry tampering of Defender driver service keys

Keywords:
BYOVD, KSLDriver.sys, Microsoft Defender, signed driver abuse, IOCTL 0x222044, ZwTerminateProcess, ZwOpenProcess, EPROCESS, ObReferenceObjectByHandle, IRP_MJ_CLEANUP, IRP_MJ_DEVICE_CONTROL, IoCreateDeviceSecure, SDDL, AllowedProcessName, DeviceName, ImagePath, PsGetCurrentProcessId, ZwQueryInformationProcess, ProcessImageFileName, DriverSiPolicy.p7b, vulnerable driver blocklist, HVCI, PPL, EDR evasion, Windows kernel driver reverse engineering